id: pm-lpqln4
title: "The bunx release gate passes with an arbitrary token in the executable position, so the only Bun coverage in the pipeline cannot fail for the reason it exists"
description: "Post-publish verification runs three executor checks. Two are correct: npx --yes <pkg> --version invokes the default binary, and npx --yes --package <pkg> -- pm --version selects the named binary explicitly. The third is not: bunx --bun <pkg> pm --version passes pm as an argument to the already-selected default binary, where it is parsed as a command name. It only succeeds because --version is handled before command dispatch. Reproduced live against the published 2026.7.26: substituting a nonsense token for pm — bunx --bun @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.26 zzzbogus --version — prints the version and exits 0. The check therefore asserts nothing about which executable bunx resolved, and nothing about whether any command dispatches under Bun. Separately, the pipeline never exercises an install path for any manager: no global install, no project install, no bun add, no pnpm add. Bun compatibility is meanwhile real but undeclared — bun add @unbrained/pm-cli links all three binaries and pm init, pm create, and pm list all succeed under the Bun runtime, while the package declares only a Node engine range and no gate re-checks that."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[6]: bun,ci,gates,install,release,verification
created_at: "2026-07-26T10:47:22.640Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-31T21:33:28.073Z"
closed_at: "2026-07-31T21:33:26.937Z"
completed_at: "2026-07-31T21:33:26.937Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 180
acceptance_criteria: "The Bun check invokes the pm executable through an explicit selection mechanism, so a wrong or missing binary fails the check; A negative-control fixture proves the check fails when the executable is absent or misnamed, and that fixture runs in the pipeline rather than existing as documentation; The verified command exercises real dispatch rather than a flag handled before dispatch, so a runtime incompatibility surfaces; Install paths are verified alongside execution paths for every supported manager, including the global install that has produced field defects; The supported runtimes are stated explicitly in package metadata and documentation, and whatever is stated is what the pipeline verifies"
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make the published-artifact checks capable of failing for the conditions they claim to cover
value: "A gate that cannot fail is worse than no gate, because it is counted as coverage. This one guards the surface users touch first — the command that runs the tool immediately after publication."
impact: "A broken Bun entrypoint, a missing binary link, or a Bun-incompatible runtime change would publish and be advertised with every check green"
outcome: "Bun and npm are verified by execution and by installation, each with a control proving the check can fail"
why_now: "The daily release advertises on these checks, and the defect is a one-line invocation shape that costs nothing to correct"
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: 90
resolution: "Replaced the inert trailing-token/version probe with explicit Bun package execution and semantic contracts dispatch, shared with contained npm/Bun install verification and negative controls."
expected_result: "Bun coverage selects the intended executable, exercises command dispatch, verifies install paths, and fails for an absent or misnamed binary."
actual_result: The release verifier performs semantic dispatch through explicit npx/bunx and installed executables; missing-bin controls fail as intended and all local/live gates pass.
