comments[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T17:09:56.506Z","harness:claude-code","Functional counter-evidence recorded 2026-07-26 against the published 2026.7.26 artifact, using a real command sequence instead of a version short-circuit. In an isolated directory with sandboxed PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH, bunx --bun @unbrained/pm-cli@latest ran init (exit 0, tracker created with prefix demo), then create (exit 0, returned id demo-jfxf), then list (exit 0, rendered the row). npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@latest also resolved and executed. So Bun genuinely works end to end on the published tarball — the defect this item owns is entirely that the gate cannot observe it, not that the capability is missing. That distinction matters for the fix: the acceptance test should be exactly this create-then-read sequence under both runtimes, because it fails closed when resolution breaks, whereas any --version probe passes even with a bogus executable token."
  "2026-07-28T10:32:19.258Z","harness:claude-code","Verification (2026-07-28, against published 2026.7.28, bun 1.x, isolated HOME): Bun compatibility is real and now demonstrated beyond `--version`, which is the gap this item describes.\n\n- `bunx @unbrained/pm-cli@latest --version` → `2026.7.28`\n- `bunx @unbrained/pm-cli@latest init --yes` → completed, full settings envelope emitted\n- `bunx @unbrained/pm-cli@latest create --title \"bun smoke\" --type Task` → `id: \"pm-9hgh\"`, `status: \"open\"`, `changed_field_count: 10`, exit 0\n\nSo a real command does dispatch under the Bun runtime — the thing the current gate asserts nothing about. npx is also green end-to-end (`npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@latest --version` → `2026.7.28`).\n\nThat sharpens rather than weakens the item: Bun works, the gate still cannot prove it, and the package still declares only a Node engine range. A replacement gate should assert on a dispatched command's output, not on `--version`, since `--version` is handled before command dispatch and is exactly why the arbitrary-token substitution passes.\n"
  "2026-07-28T15:00:24.229Z","harness:claude-code","Published-artifact acceptance re-verified 2026-07-28 at version 2026.7.28: npx -y and bunx --bun both print the version and exit 0. Still only exercises the short-circuiting --version path, which is exactly the gate weakness this item owns, so this is a freshness datapoint and not evidence the gate can fail."
  "2026-07-28T22:38:58.454Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence 2026-07-28 against this item's second half, that no install path is exercised for any manager. Both runners were driven through a real workflow rather than a short-circuiting --version, in separate clean sandboxes with isolated PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH, against published 2026.7.28:\n\n  npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@latest --version   -> 2026.7.28\n  npx ... init                                -> structured init result\n  npx ... create --title \"npx smoke item\" --type Task --status open --json -> id pm-ckmg, changed_field_count 10\n  npx ... list-open --fields id,title          -> returns pm-ckmg, count 1\n\n  bunx --bun @unbrained/pm-cli@latest --version -> 2026.7.28, under bun 1.3.11\n  bunx ... init                                 -> structured init result\n  bunx ... create --title \"bunx smoke item\" ... -> id pm-c4sm, changed_field_count 10\n  bunx ... list-open --fields id,title           -> returns pm-c4sm\n\nSo the artifact genuinely works under both runners on a real create-then-read sequence. That does not weaken this item: the defect it owns is that the GATE cannot fail, proved by zzzbogus --version passing, and a manual verification each pass is exactly the substitute for a gate that this item exists to remove. The evidence above is what the gate should assert automatically, and it should assert the create-then-read sequence rather than --version, since --version short-circuits before any command dispatch.\n"
  "2026-07-29T04:42:14.806Z","harness:claude-code","Re-proved live against the published 2026.7.29 artifact on 2026-07-29, and the false positive is now demonstrated to extend past --version. Running npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.29 pm --version prints 2026.7.29 and exits 0, exactly as this item predicts, because --version short-circuits before command resolution. The same invocation form with real work fails: pm init reports attempted_command pm pm init, and pm list-open reports Error: Unknown command pm with the explanation that pm does not expose command path pm. So the published bin is pm and the trailing pm is parsed as a subcommand; every gate written in the package-then-pm form validates nothing beyond the short-circuit. The correct form was then verified end to end on both runtimes: npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.29 followed directly by init, create and list-open succeeds, and bunx @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.29 on bun 1.3.11 succeeds identically. The bunx negative control is clean - a bogus subcommand fails with Unknown command zzzbogus - so the artifact itself is healthy on both package managers and only the gate's invocation form is wrong. Any acceptance step for this item must assert a real subcommand and must assert that a bogus subcommand fails, since a passing --version proves nothing."
  "2026-07-29T17:04:17.462Z","harness:claude-code","Published-artifact acceptance re-verified 2026-07-29 against npm version 2026.7.29, and one new integration hazard measured.\n\nBoth managers run the real published artifact end to end, not just a short-circuiting version flag. npx with the latest tag: version 2026.7.29, init, create and list-open all succeed against a sandbox tracker, list-open reporting count 1 for the item just created. bunx with the bun runtime flag, bun 1.3.11: version 2026.7.29, init, create, next and list-open all succeed.\n\nNew hazard, and it belongs to this item's install-path scope rather than to any pm command. On a cold cache bunx writes its own progress to standard output before the command runs - three lines, \"Resolving dependencies\", \"Resolved, downloaded and extracted [2]\", \"Saved lockfile\" - so any documented bunx one-liner that pipes a pm json read into a parser fails on first invocation with a decode error at line 1. Redirecting standard error does not help because the chatter is on standard output. pm's own output is clean and correctly separated; the wrapper pollutes the stream. The acceptance stage should therefore either warm the cache in a preceding step or assert on a byte offset rather than on the whole stream, and any bunx example published in the docs needs the same treatment.\n"
  "2026-07-29T21:26:18.609Z","harness:codex","Live verification 2026-07-29 after the ecosystem review: npm reports 2026.7.29, GitHub exposes v2026.7.29 as the sole release for the UTC day, the scheduled Auto Release run succeeded, and a later workflow_dispatch completed without producing a second tag. The package-owned published verifier passed npm metadata, direct npx, package-selected npx, bunx, and GitHub Release in one attempt each. Exact-head main CI, CodeQL, Docs, Security and Script Quality, CodSpeed, and OSSF Scorecard are green at 4323c7e15ca3303eff9914b63ca9340ea660b434. This does not close this issue: the verifier's bunx shape still needs a real dispatched command and a failing bogus-command or missing-binary control so the gate proves it can fail."
  "2026-07-31T21:33:21.335Z","harness:codex","Delivery evidence 2026-07-31: Bun verification now selects the package executable explicitly, executes the real contracts command instead of the pre-dispatch --version short circuit, and verifies a contained Bun-installed binary. The same semantic dispatch runs through npx and direct installed paths. Negative controls prove a missing/misnamed executable fails. Runtime and invocation expectations are documented in docs/RELEASING.md. Focused verifier tests, the full 6,623-test suite, exact 100/100/100/100 coverage, and live npm+npx+bunx verification of 2026.7.31 all pass."
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T11:33:06.040Z","harness:claude-code","Positive control for the gate this item says cannot fail, run 2026-07-26 against the real published artifact rather than a bogus token.\n\n  npx -y @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.26 --version   ->  2026.7.26\n  bunx @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.7.26 --version     ->  2026.7.26\n\nBoth resolve, download, and execute the real package under their own runtime, from a clean temp directory with no prior cache. So the capability is genuinely there — which is exactly what makes the gate's weakness matter: a gate that also passes for zzzbogus is indistinguishable from this run, and this run is the outcome the gate is supposed to be asserting. Having both the true positive and the known false positive recorded on the same item gives the fix a two-sided test: the replacement gate must pass here and fail on the bogus name.\n\nAlso relevant to the item's second half, that no install path is exercised for any manager: neither of these invocations installs anything persistent. A real install-path assertion still has no coverage."
  "2026-07-31T19:41:58.044Z","harness:claude-code","Mechanism confirmed empirically 2026-07-31 against the shipped build, so the fix does not have to re-derive why the token is inert.\n\n    $ node dist/cli.js ZZZ_NOT_A_COMMAND --version\n    2026.7.31\n    exit=0\n\n--version is resolved before subcommand dispatch, so any token in that position is discarded and the exit code stays 0. The bunx probe in scripts/release/verify-published-release.mjs:180-187 invokes\n\n    bunx --bun @unbrained/pm-cli@<version> pm --version\n\nwhere pm sits in the argument position of the resolved default bin rather than naming a bin. The assertion then compares lastNonEmptyLine(stdout) against the version, which the short-circuit satisfies. So the probe is green regardless of whether the pm bin resolves, exists, or works.\n\nContrast with the npx pair immediately above it, which is constructed correctly: npx-package uses --package <spec> -- pm --version, where pm genuinely selects a bin. The bunx call is not the same shape as its npx sibling, which is why the defect reads as a typo rather than a design choice.\n\nOne consequence worth carrying into the fix. Because --version short-circuits, a --version assertion cannot distinguish a working entrypoint from a dead one for ANY bin, not just under bunx. That is why pm-u0oz2k - filed today for the pm-mcp entrypoint having zero release coverage - requires a JSON-RPC initialize handshake rather than a version probe. The two items share a root cause: the pipeline's liveness assertions are satisfied by a code path that runs before the thing being asserted. Fixing the bunx argument order here removes the inert token; it does not by itself make the probe capable of detecting a dead bin."
